# The check after the read emits no code, because it is split in two. A type test that covers the full type takes every value that is not nullish, and on its failing edge a check covers the rest. Together they check what the single check did.
# The injected fault treats what the program passes to unknown code as non-escaping. Link does not see the store of the function that the host passes, and keeps claiming what the array held at first.
# RUN: run-in-shell mechanisms=all globalThis.put = (list, value) => { list[0] = value; };
# CHECK: 3
# CHECK-NEXT: undefined
# CHECK-NEXT: went on
# CHECK-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(SHAPE): walk f1 mechanisms=all testing=AssumeArgumentsNeverEscape
# SHAPE: CheckProof {{*}}source=Contents [isLeftToTestBehind]
# SHAPE: TypeTest {{*}}[asksInFull]
# SHAPE: CheckProof {{*}}source=Contents
# RUN(FAULT): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=AssumeArgumentsNeverEscape globalThis.put = (list, value) => { list[0] = value; };
# FAULT: 3
# FAULT: found int32 in an element
# FAULT-NOT: went on
# FAULT: crashed
